---
import Layout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<Layout title="How the Model Is Computed | Rex Autistikōn Labs">
  <main class="md-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="kicker">Interactive Model · Documentation</p>
      <h1>How the model is computed</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        Phase 1 didactic transmission layer. This page explains what the interactive model
        does—and what it must not be mistaken for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <div class="banner">
        <strong>Phase 1 — illustrative only.</strong>
        Nothing on the interactive model page is a measurement. Adjustable controls
        (including any restriction-like slider) stand in for a <em>hypothesis-level latent
        mechanical state</em>, not an instrument reading.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Place in the research hierarchy</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Measurement layer</strong> — observed mechanical quantities (zone-specific instruments/protocols).</li>
        <li><strong>Mechanical representation layer</strong> — derived parameters (\(G'\), \(G''\), strain, hysteresis, relaxation, displacement, force–displacement, etc.).</li>
        <li><strong>Computational transmission layer</strong> — <em>this model</em>: predicted transmission quantities.</li>
        <li><strong>Physiological test layer</strong> — independent outcomes under preregistered analyses.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Full program language:
        <a href="/research-framework">Research Framework</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What the interactive model does</h2>
      <p>
        Given a stated mechanical representation (for example stiffness- and viscosity-like
        terms in a Kelvin–Voigt-style element), the model computes predicted quantities such as:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>transmitted amplitude / fidelity-like proxies</li>
        <li>effective precision proxies (π<sub>eff</sub>-style terms)</li>
        <li>prediction-error terms</li>
        <li>free-energy-style costs and illustrative gradients with respect to didactic controls</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Exact equations and defaults are versioned with the calculation engine
        (<code>model_version</code> on stored runs).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What “restriction” means in the UI</h2>
      <p>
        Any restriction-like control is a <strong>didactic latent variable</strong> used to
        explore model sensitivity. In empirical work, latent mechanical state must be approached
        through the measurement and representation layers. Sliding a control is not data acquisition.
      </p>
      <p>
        MPA-style overlays, if shown, are observational—not validated mechanical gold standards
        and not primary determinants of computational parameters until preregistered validity criteria are met.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Low-dimensional; not full soft-tissue, fluid, or neural circuit physics.</li>
        <li>Parameters and constitutive assumptions are provisional.</li>
        <li>Zone anatomy differs; one illustrative control law does not imply one physical instrument for all eight interfaces.</li>
        <li>Not for diagnosis, triage, or treatment decisions.</li>
        <li>Empirical support for a latent “restriction” construct is to be tested, not presupposed by the interface.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Falsification (scientific vs software)</h2>
      <p>
        Scientific falsification of the restriction construct is defined in the Research Framework
        (reliability failure, construct failure, predictive failure). Internal UI sensitivity
        (e.g. how predicted metrics move when an illustrative control changes) is
        <strong>software quality assurance only</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block end">
      <p>
        <a href="/model">Open interactive model →</a>
        ·
        <a href="/research-framework">Research Framework →</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .md-page { background: #f8fafc; color: #0f172a; }
  .hero {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0f766e 0%, #134e4a 100%);
    color: #fff; padding: 3rem 1.25rem 2.5rem; text-align: center;
  }
  .kicker {
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; font-size: 0.75rem;
    opacity: 0.85; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-weight: 600;
  }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.1rem); font-weight: 700; margin: 0 auto 0.75rem; }
  .subtitle { max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; line-height: 1.55; opacity: 0.92; }
  .block { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.75rem 1.25rem 0.25rem; }
  .block h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 0.7rem; color: #0f172a;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #ccfbf1; padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .block p, .block li { line-height: 1.65; color: #334155; }
  .banner {
    background: #fef3c7; border: 1px solid #fcd34d; border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem; color: #78350f; line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .block a { color: #0f766e; font-weight: 600; }
  code { font-size: 0.85em; background: #f1f5f9; padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem; border-radius: 4px; }
  .end { padding-bottom: 3.5rem; }
</style>
